1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:11,010
In the first world war, and for the 
first time, in the history of man,

2
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:16,520
nations combined to fight against nations, 
using the crude weapons of those days.

3
00:00:16,970 --> 00:00:20,060
The second world war 
involved every continent on the globe,

4
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:23,440
and men turned to science 
for new devices of warfare,

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,400
which reached an unparalleled peak 
in their capacity for destruction.

6
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:31,360
And now, fought with the terrible 
weapons of super science,

7
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:36,240
menacing all mankind and every creature 
on earth, comes...

8
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:42,420
The war of the worlds

9
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:45,560
No one would have believed 
in the middle of the 20th century,

10
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:49,270
that human affairs 
were being watched keenly and closely

11
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:52,530
by intelligences greater than man's.

12
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:56,490
Yet., across the Gulf of space, 
on the planet, Mars,

13
00:01:56,570 --> 00:01:59,660
intellects vast and cool and unsympathetic

14
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:02,750
regarded our earth with envious eyes,

15
00:02:02,830 --> 00:02:06,920
slowly and surely, 
drawing their plans against us.

16
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:12,010
Mars is more than 140 million miles 
from the sun,

17
00:02:12,090 --> 00:02:15,970
and for centuries, it has been 
in the last stages of exhaustion.

18
00:02:16,090 --> 00:02:20,060
At night, temperatures 
drop far below zero,

19
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:22,180
even at its equator.

20
00:02:22,270 --> 00:02:24,390
The inhabitants of this dying planet

21
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:27,150
looked across space 
with instruments and intelligences

22
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:29,980
of which we have scarcely dreamed,

23
00:02:30,070 --> 00:02:34,150
searching for another world 
to which they could migrate.

24
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:38,280
They could not go to pluto, 
outermost of all the planets,

25
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:42,290
and so cold, that its atmosphere 
lies frozen on its surface.

26
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,420
They couldn't go to Neptune or uranus,

27
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:50,420
twin worlds in eternal night 
and perpetual cold,

28
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,210
both surrounded by 
an unbreathable atmosphere

29
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:55,880
of methane gas and ammonia vapor.

30
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,470
The martians considered saturn,

31
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:04,600
an attractive world with its many moons 
and beautiful rings of cosmic dust,

32
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:10,310
but its temperature is close 
to 270 degrees below zero,

33
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:15,030
and ice lies 15,000 miles 
deep on its surface.

34
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:17,570
Their nearest world was giant Jupiter,

35
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:20,620
where there are Titanic cliffs 
of lava and ice,

36
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,370
with hydrogen flaming at the tops,

37
00:03:23,450 --> 00:03:25,040
where the atmospheric pressure 
is terrible,

38
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:29,120
thousands of pounds to the square inch.

39
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:30,960
They couldn't go there.

40
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:34,420
Nor could they go to Mercury, 
nearest planet to the sun.

41
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:36,090
It has no air,

42
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:40,470
and the temperature at its equator 
is that of molten lead.

43
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:42,680
Of all the worlds,

44
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:46,810
that the intelligences on Mars 
could see and study,

45
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:49,770
only our own warm earth

46
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:53,650
was green with vegetation, 
bright with water,

47
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:58,070
and possessed a cloudy atmosphere 
eloquent of fertility.

48
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:04,030
It did not occur to mankind that 
a swift fate might be hanging over us,

49
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:07,080
or that from the blackness of outer space,

50
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:10,710
we were being scrutinized and studied,

51
00:04:10,790 --> 00:04:13,670
until at the time of our nearest approach

52
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:16,710
to the orbit of Mars, 
during a pleasant summer season...

53
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:33,060
- Is that a fireball, or something? 
- Boy, that's big!

54
00:04:33,150 --> 00:04:34,520
Maybe it's a comet.

55
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:39,190
Boy, that had me scared.

56
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:40,400
I wonder where it lit.

57
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:42,450
- Miles away, I bet you. 
- Hey, let's go find it, huh?

58
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:44,030
That probably dropped halfway to pomona.

59
00:04:44,070 --> 00:04:46,830
- It was nearer than that. 
- I'm gonna see. Who's coming?

60
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:52,540
- This is pine summit. I got a smoke. 
- 160, 30.

61
00:04:52,670 --> 00:04:56,040
Azimuth reading: 160 degrees, 30 minutes.

62
00:04:56,710 --> 00:05:01,430
Big? I'll bet it's as big as anything 
that ever lit in California.

63
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:04,850
You better get something over there. 
It's started a Blaze already.

64
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:13,270
Over here. Hey, let's go this way.

65
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:14,900
You there, with the shovel!

66
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:29,580
Let me get in there!

67
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:31,160
Out of the way! Out of the way!

68
00:05:34,330 --> 00:05:36,380
Bring that hose over here!

69
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:39,050
We're getting it licked, fellas. 
Keep at it.

70
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:40,920
Hey, grab this.

71
00:05:41,050 --> 00:05:43,300
Joe, Joe, lend a hand over here.

72
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:47,550
Number three to d.O. Number three to d.O.

73
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:49,970
D.o. To number three, come in.

74
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:53,390
We're getting this under control. 
We won't need any more help. Over.

75
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:54,850
Okay. Send the tanker in,

76
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:58,650
but you can stand by 
until that thing cools off. Over.

77
00:05:58,730 --> 00:06:01,030
I think somebody ought 
to check on it. Over.

78
00:06:01,110 --> 00:06:03,240
Well, there's some fellows 
fishing at pine summit.

79
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:04,820
I think they're scientists.

80
00:06:04,910 --> 00:06:08,990
They probably saw it come down. 
I'll let 'em know. What's it look like?

81
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:12,830
Can't get near enough 
to see it very well. It's too hot.

82
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:14,960
But it's a whale of a size.

83
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:25,380
I got a message for you.

84
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:28,140
You're the guys 
from pacific tech, ain't you?

85
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:29,260
Right.

86
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:32,390
Looks like the fishing was good.

87
00:06:32,470 --> 00:06:34,140
Have some?

88
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:40,820
It's about that meteor.

89
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:44,190
They say it's a whopper.

90
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:49,780
The district officer phoned us 
at the lookout up on the summit.

91
00:06:49,950 --> 00:06:51,580
I thought you might be interested.

92
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:54,160
It's about 10 or 12 miles from here, 
over by Linda Rosa.

93
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:56,870
Are they sure it's a meteor? 
It didn't come down like one.

94
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:59,750
That's right. 
Came down in kind of spurts, didn't it?

95
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:05,010
Well, you fellas will have 
to figure it out. You're scientists.

96
00:07:05,090 --> 00:07:08,640
All I know is, they say it's as big 
as a house, and practically red-hot.

97
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:11,350
I'd like to borrow your car 
and take a look at it in the morning.

98
00:07:11,430 --> 00:07:12,510
We ought to get back.

99
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,350
- I can fly bilderbeck down in your plane. 
- Okay.

100
00:07:15,430 --> 00:07:17,390
The insurance is paid up.

101
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:21,060
- Light? 
- No, I'll smoke it later.

102
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,210
- Did you watch it come down? 
- I seen it from my window.

103
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:45,090
Harold. Harold. Look here a minute.

104
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:48,090
Step over to your left. 
Just a little and hold it.

105
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:49,260
Smile.

106
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:52,640
It must've hit way up there, 
and then skidded along the gully.

107
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,810
After it stopped, all that loose earth 
and stuff shook down over it.

108
00:07:56,020 --> 00:07:58,440
- Excuse me. 
- I guess most of it's buried.

109
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:00,690
That's 12 feet thick, easy. Maybe more.

110
00:08:00,770 --> 00:08:02,480
Meteors always run heavy.

111
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:06,280
They won't be able to haul this one 
away to no museum.

112
00:08:06,610 --> 00:08:09,200
It'll be a real good attraction 
for Sunday drivers.

113
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:13,370
Better than a lion farm, or a snake pit. 
We won't have to feed it.

114
00:08:13,450 --> 00:08:16,580
Sure. We can sell the tamales, 
and the enchiladas, and the hot dogs, too!

115
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:18,410
Yeah! Ice cream, cold drinks, souvenirs.

116
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:21,000
I think we should put up 
a few picnic tables.

117
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,750
No, no. Then they'd bring 
their own lunches.

118
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:26,510
What's the idea, buck?

119
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:29,510
- Gonna dig for gold? 
- You think you're kidding?

120
00:08:29,590 --> 00:08:32,840
It's gonna be like having a gold mine 
in our own backyard.

121
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:36,640
I'm gonna get a closer look at it.

122
00:08:42,310 --> 00:08:44,610
Reckon it's solid all through?

123
00:08:44,690 --> 00:08:47,320
Lucky it didn't come down 
in the middle of town!

124
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,150
I wonder who owns this property.

125
00:08:51,150 --> 00:08:52,280
That's it, over there.

126
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:55,780
- What are you gonna do... 
- Feels pretty darn hot!

127
00:08:55,870 --> 00:08:57,490
What's the idea of the shovel?

128
00:08:57,660 --> 00:08:59,330
- Be careful, buck. 
- Yeah, watch it, buck.

129
00:09:02,670 --> 00:09:03,830
It's pretty hot there.

130
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:09,550
- Did you see it come down? 
- Yes. I was fishing up in the hills.

131
00:09:09,630 --> 00:09:11,680
Well, you must've caught 
plenty with all that tackle.

132
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:15,010
There were three of us. 
The others flew back in my plane.

133
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:19,520
I don't understand why a meteor that size 
didn't make a bigger crater.

134
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:21,640
It hit sideways, and skidded in.

135
00:09:21,730 --> 00:09:24,440
At least that's what I think. 
I don't really know.

136
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:27,070
But the ranger said a scientist 
is coming from pacific tech.

137
00:09:27,150 --> 00:09:28,230
He'll tell us.

138
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,360
Clayton forrester. Ever hear of him?

139
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,700
What's that fellow 
trying to do over there, dig it out?

140
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:36,700
He's top man in astro and nuclear physics. 
He knows all about meteors.

141
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:39,700
You seem to know all about him.

142
00:09:39,950 --> 00:09:44,170
Well, I did a thesis on modern scientists, 
working for my master's degree.

143
00:09:44,290 --> 00:09:47,670
- Did it do you any good? 
- Why, sure. I got it.

144
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:53,970
- Say, do you have a match? 
- No, I'm sorry. I don't smoke.

145
00:09:54,050 --> 00:09:56,510
Forrester's the man 
behind the new atomic engines.

146
00:09:56,640 --> 00:09:58,260
They had him on the cover of time.

147
00:09:58,350 --> 00:10:00,680
You know, you've got to rate to get that.

148
00:10:00,770 --> 00:10:02,100
He isn't that good.

149
00:10:02,390 --> 00:10:04,690
Well, now, how can you 
say that when you don't even know him?

150
00:10:04,730 --> 00:10:07,150
I do know him. Slightly.

151
00:10:07,230 --> 00:10:10,820
- What's he like? 
- Well, he's like...

152
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:12,190
Like...

153
00:10:16,910 --> 00:10:20,240
Well, you certainly don't look like 
yourself in that getup, Dr. forrester,

154
00:10:20,330 --> 00:10:22,250
but I'm happy to meet you, anyway.

155
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:25,540
I'm Sylvia Van buren. 
I teach library science over at usc.

156
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:27,330
I didn't know how to stop you.

157
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:29,630
Well, I might have 
recognized you without the beard,

158
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:31,880
and you didn't wear glasses 
on the time cover.

159
00:10:32,010 --> 00:10:33,340
They're really for long distance.

160
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:36,550
When I want to look at something close, 
I take them off.

161
00:10:52,530 --> 00:10:54,990
Boy, you could fry eggs on that thing!

162
00:10:55,070 --> 00:10:57,740
All that sand will keep the heat in 
for a long time.

163
00:10:57,820 --> 00:11:00,530
Uncle Matthew, 
this is Dr. Clayton forrester.

164
00:11:00,620 --> 00:11:03,620
My uncle, Dr. Matthew Collins, 
pastor of the community church.

165
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:07,460
- Well, I... how do you do, Dr. forrester? 
- How do you do, sir?

166
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:09,540
Hey, you!

167
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:19,840
What you got in here, fella? 
Ticking like a bomb.

168
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:26,230
This is a geiger counter 
for detecting radioactivity.

169
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:29,900
We did a little bit of surveying 
while we were up in the hills.

170
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:41,570
It's that meteor.

171
00:11:42,530 --> 00:11:43,700
It's radioactive?

172
00:11:43,830 --> 00:11:47,960
Yes. Difficult to account 
for a reaction like that.

173
00:11:52,710 --> 00:11:54,880
Look at this thing. It's going crazy.

174
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:56,970
Maybe we ought to keep people 
away from it, huh?

175
00:11:57,050 --> 00:11:58,510
Might be a good idea.

176
00:11:58,590 --> 00:11:59,970
I'll post two or three deputies.

177
00:12:00,050 --> 00:12:02,260
They can make sure 
it doesn't start any more fires, too.

178
00:12:03,050 --> 00:12:05,060
You know, that meteor's either very light,

179
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:07,930
which is unheard of, 
or else it's hollow somehow.

180
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:09,890
If it were heavy and solid,

181
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:12,730
it would have made 
a tremendous crater when it landed.

182
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:16,940
I think I'll wait around 
until it cools off.

183
00:12:16,990 --> 00:12:20,200
If you tell me of a place in town, 
I'd like to clean up.

184
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:22,910
I'd be delighted if you'd stay 
at my house, Dr. forrester.

185
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:24,580
Thank you.

186
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:27,830
Probably won't be cool 
for another 24 hours.

187
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:29,080
What do people do around here 
on a Saturday?

188
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:31,500
They don't do much of anything.

189
00:12:31,580 --> 00:12:34,000
There's a square dance 
at the social hall this evening.

190
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:09,080
Well, let's take another look at it.

191
00:13:36,230 --> 00:13:37,730
Almost cold now, ain't it?

192
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:40,440
It won't start any more fires. 
We might as well go home.

193
00:13:40,530 --> 00:13:42,900
Yeah, no sense staying out here.

194
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:44,660
Let's go.

195
00:13:49,830 --> 00:13:52,710
Hey! It's moving!

196
00:14:10,310 --> 00:14:12,100
It's a bomb.

197
00:14:12,180 --> 00:14:14,190
It don't go off last night.

198
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:16,020
Maybe it's gonna go off now, huh?

199
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:18,020
It's an enemy sneak attack.

200
00:14:18,190 --> 00:14:21,230
- Let's get out of here. 
- Wait a minute. Wait a minute.

201
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:26,160
Bombs don't unscrew.

202
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:29,280
It's no meteor, that's for sure.

203
00:14:31,160 --> 00:14:34,210
Darnedest thing I ever saw.

204
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:35,960
The way that's unscrewing.

205
00:14:44,970 --> 00:14:47,220
Thank you, Dr. forrester. You having fun?

206
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:50,430
Yep. And you want to know 
what I was thinking?

207
00:14:50,510 --> 00:14:53,640
If we could gather all the energy 
expended in just one square dance,

208
00:14:53,730 --> 00:14:55,980
we could send that meteor 
back to where it came from.

209
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:47,610
- Must be somebody in there. 
- Who?

210
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:49,910
- Where do you think they come from? 
- How would I know?

211
00:15:50,030 --> 00:15:53,620
I read someplace 
Mars is near the earth right now.

212
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:57,040
Happens every 18, 20 years, they say.

213
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:59,830
Men from Mars.

214
00:15:58,080 --> 00:16:01,500
What do you think?

215
00:16:01,580 --> 00:16:04,710
Maybe these are not men, not like us.

216
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:08,050
Everything human 
doesn't have to look like you and me.

217
00:16:12,550 --> 00:16:16,720
If it's men from Mars, 
we ought to let them know we're friendly.

218
00:16:16,810 --> 00:16:19,770
Don't fool around with something 
when you don't know what it is.

219
00:16:19,810 --> 00:16:22,480
We'd be the first to make 
contact with them, see?

220
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:24,730
We'd be in all the papers.

221
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:26,110
How about that?

222
00:16:26,190 --> 00:16:27,650
We could show them we're friendly, huh?

223
00:16:27,690 --> 00:16:29,860
Walk out there with a white flag.

224
00:16:30,450 --> 00:16:33,240
Hey, I got an old sugar sack in my car.

225
00:16:42,330 --> 00:16:45,000
What are we going to say to them?

226
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:49,630
"Welcome to California.”

227
00:16:49,720 --> 00:16:51,590
Come on.

228
00:17:22,290 --> 00:17:26,040
We're friends! Yeah!

229
00:17:26,130 --> 00:17:28,340
Hey, there! Open up!

230
00:17:28,420 --> 00:17:32,090
- How they gonna understand us? 
- We'll talk in sign language.

231
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:36,510
- They'll understand us, all right. 
- Sure. Sure!

232
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:38,810
Everybody understands 
when you wave the white flag,

233
00:17:38,890 --> 00:17:40,480
you wanna be friends.

234
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,730
Hey, there! Open up!

235
00:17:42,810 --> 00:17:44,560
Come on out! We're friends!

236
00:17:44,650 --> 00:17:47,360
That's right! We welcome you!

237
00:17:47,570 --> 00:17:49,230
We're friends! Yeah!

238
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:56,490
- Hey! Hey! 
- Hey! Hey!

239
00:18:02,870 --> 00:18:04,710
Where's the fuse box?

240
00:18:04,790 --> 00:18:06,460
No smooching in the dark, folks.

241
00:18:09,300 --> 00:18:10,760
Any candles around?

242
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:13,840
Hey, look! Look! 
All the lights in town have gone out!

243
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:20,560
Somebody's found some candles.

244
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:22,180
- Zippy? 
- T like it in the dark.

245
00:18:22,270 --> 00:18:23,810
Call the electric company 
and see what happened.

246
00:18:23,850 --> 00:18:25,140
Okay.

247
00:18:35,910 --> 00:18:39,620
- Hey! The phone's gone dead! 
- That's funny.

248
00:18:39,700 --> 00:18:41,990
The phone's not on the same circuit 
as the lights.

249
00:18:42,370 --> 00:18:44,080
What are they saying, honey? 
Something's wrong with my hearing aid.

250
00:18:47,170 --> 00:18:50,130
Well, we always play goodnight, ladies 
at 12:00, anyway.

251
00:18:50,210 --> 00:18:52,420
It must be nearly that now.

252
00:18:54,260 --> 00:18:57,300
- My watch has stopped. 
- I've got the time.

253
00:18:59,430 --> 00:19:02,430
- Mine stopped, too. 
- So has mine.

254
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:04,730
Jeepers, mine ain't working. What is this?

255
00:19:09,110 --> 00:19:11,940
Why, they've all stopped at the same time.

256
00:19:12,020 --> 00:19:15,360
There's only one explanation 
for a thing like this.

257
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:17,450
Do you have a pin?

258
00:19:27,670 --> 00:19:30,460
- Look! 
- Look!

259
00:19:30,540 --> 00:19:34,170
See that? 
My watch is magnetized.

260
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:39,800
How could it happen 
to everybody's watch altogether?

261
00:19:39,840 --> 00:19:42,970
- Do you have a pocket compass? 
- Yeah.

262
00:19:47,230 --> 00:19:49,730
Hey, that needle isn't pointing north!

263
00:19:49,810 --> 00:19:53,150
It's pointing to the gully 
where that meteor came down.

264
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:55,940
All right, folks, let me through. 
Let me through here.

265
00:20:06,700 --> 00:20:07,960
Sheriff, what's going on?

266
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:10,790
I don't know any more than you do, Joe.

267
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:12,670
Look at the fire out there.

268
00:20:16,340 --> 00:20:18,220
Let's go see.

269
00:20:45,740 --> 00:20:49,960
The power lines are down. 
That explains why the lights went out.

270
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:55,670
What the... hey, look at the car!

271
00:20:55,800 --> 00:20:58,920
Where are the three men you left out here?

272
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:03,800
Look therel

273
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:32,080
Jump! Get under cover!

274
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:49,220
What is that gizmo?

275
00:21:49,310 --> 00:21:53,140
I think that gizmo is a machine 
from another planet.

276
00:21:54,230 --> 00:21:56,940
We better get word to the authorities.

277
00:21:57,400 --> 00:21:59,190
Look!

278
00:22:02,950 --> 00:22:06,910
Sheriff, you'd better 
get word to the military.

279
00:22:06,950 --> 00:22:09,620
You're going to need them out here.

280
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:31,020
Get moving, on the double!

281
00:22:32,100 --> 00:22:35,940
Take cover along the riverbed! 
Get those .30s over here!

282
00:22:39,480 --> 00:22:41,820
The area is under control 
of marines from El toro base,

283
00:22:41,900 --> 00:22:43,150
and the gully is surrounded.

284
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:44,450
And here's professor mcpherson

285
00:22:44,490 --> 00:22:46,860
of the Canadian 
meteorological research council.

286
00:22:46,950 --> 00:22:48,870
Is it true, professor, 
you've had reports of landings

287
00:22:48,910 --> 00:22:52,620
- in other places? In Canada? 
- Not in Canada yet.

288
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:55,790
But in Bordeaux, France. Some in Spain.

289
00:22:55,870 --> 00:22:59,590
There's supposed to be 
one down near the Gulf of taranto, Italy.

290
00:22:59,710 --> 00:23:01,840
We're trying to locate the second meteor

291
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:04,590
that landed in this vicinity 
just about midnight.

292
00:23:04,670 --> 00:23:07,180
Well, do you think they come from Mars?

293
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:09,640
What do you think, Dr. forrester?

294
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:11,680
It's possible. At least it seems certain

295
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:15,270
they're from some planet 
other than our own.

296
00:23:15,770 --> 00:23:18,270
Suppose they are martians, professor. 
What would they look like?

297
00:23:18,310 --> 00:23:20,270
Bigger than us? Smaller?

298
00:23:20,310 --> 00:23:21,900
Well, as to martians,

299
00:23:21,980 --> 00:23:24,530
our gravitational pull 
would weigh them down.

300
00:23:24,610 --> 00:23:26,280
Our heavier air would oppress them.

301
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:28,780
Then you think they'd be 
breathing creatures like us.

302
00:23:28,860 --> 00:23:31,240
What about the hearts 
and blood and all that?

303
00:23:31,330 --> 00:23:34,620
But if they are martians, 
and if they do have hearts,

304
00:23:34,700 --> 00:23:37,160
they'd almost certainly 
beat at a slower rate.

305
00:23:37,250 --> 00:23:39,130
Their veins might be distended.

306
00:23:39,210 --> 00:23:42,250
Their senses could be quite different 
from ours, of course.

307
00:23:42,590 --> 00:23:46,130
They may, for instance, 
be able to smell colors.

308
00:23:46,550 --> 00:23:48,050
Precedent in our own evolution

309
00:23:48,130 --> 00:23:51,350
makes it possible 
that they have more than one brain.

310
00:23:51,430 --> 00:23:54,600
You mean two? Three? 
Just think of that, folks!

311
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:56,270
It's only speculation.

312
00:23:56,350 --> 00:23:59,400
Now, Dr. forrester, 
what about these meteor machines?

313
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:02,730
They're probably controlled by jets 
after they enter our atmosphere,

314
00:24:02,820 --> 00:24:06,530
and navigated by some form 
of gyroscopic mechanism.

315
00:24:06,690 --> 00:24:08,860
Thank you, Dr. forrester.

316
00:24:08,900 --> 00:24:12,370
Colonel, can you tell us anything 
about this plane that's coming over?

317
00:24:12,450 --> 00:24:13,490
It'll drop a flare.

318
00:24:13,580 --> 00:24:15,160
That's the only way 
we dare put light on them.

319
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,250
Then air force cameramen 
will get pictures.

320
00:24:17,330 --> 00:24:19,870
That was marine colonel Ralph heffner.

321
00:24:19,960 --> 00:24:22,750
There's been a lot of mysterious activity 
around the machine.

322
00:24:22,840 --> 00:24:25,800
Lights and dust, 
as if they're digging themselves out.

323
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:27,010
And there it comes again!

324
00:24:29,510 --> 00:24:32,680
If this keeps up, it'll be a guide 
for the plane when it comes over.

325
00:24:40,770 --> 00:24:42,650
- Is that the plane, now? 
- Yes. He's signaling.

326
00:24:42,730 --> 00:24:45,570
The pilot has just blipped his motor. 
That means he's dropped the flare.

327
00:24:45,650 --> 00:24:48,280
He's flying high, so, it'll take 
a few seconds for it to come down.

328
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:50,320
When it does burst, 
we shall be the first men on earth

329
00:24:50,360 --> 00:24:53,120
to get a real look 
at these invaders from space.

330
00:25:09,470 --> 00:25:11,220
They're going after the plane 
with their ray!

331
00:25:13,090 --> 00:25:15,350
- Take cover! 
- Jump for it!

332
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:23,940
We are...

333
00:25:25,400 --> 00:25:27,610
Hey!

334
00:25:27,650 --> 00:25:29,570
They cut me off! They got my truck!

335
00:25:32,950 --> 00:25:35,740
It looks like they're gonna come out 
of that gully pretty soon.

336
00:25:35,820 --> 00:25:38,080
We'll have to rush our defenses 
to be ready when they do.

337
00:25:38,200 --> 00:25:39,660
You're going to need 
plenty of reinforcements.

338
00:25:39,700 --> 00:25:40,080
We'll get them.

339
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:27,840
They got the fire road blocked.

340
00:26:27,920 --> 00:26:29,500
A truck turned over.

341
00:26:30,300 --> 00:26:32,090
The troops are certainly moving in here.

342
00:26:32,210 --> 00:26:34,380
I know you sent word 
to the sixth army command.

343
00:26:34,470 --> 00:26:37,550
No, I just told them the local situation. 
Colonel heffner's in charge.

344
00:26:41,310 --> 00:26:42,560
You never know where 
you're going to wind up

345
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:44,100
when you go to a square dance, do you?

346
00:26:44,140 --> 00:26:45,690
Runner? Where's a runner?

347
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:47,690
- That's d-24. 
- Correct.

348
00:26:49,980 --> 00:26:53,320
Now, locate your 
observation post on this hill.

349
00:26:53,360 --> 00:26:57,070
Position your recoilless 75s 
back here, carbon canyon.

350
00:26:57,200 --> 00:26:59,870
- Yes, sir. 
- I want your battery here.

351
00:26:59,910 --> 00:27:01,660
You may find at daylight, 
you're too exposed,

352
00:27:01,700 --> 00:27:04,120
so, keep your prime movers 
ready to pull you out fast.

353
00:27:04,200 --> 00:27:05,620
But you'll get first crack at them.

354
00:27:05,710 --> 00:27:07,290
- Suits me. Will that be all, sir? 
- Colonel heffner?

355
00:27:07,330 --> 00:27:09,340
Yes. Report when you're setup.

356
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:13,760
Heffner.

357
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:17,090
Half-tracks? Okay.

358
00:27:17,220 --> 00:27:19,350
Get in back of hill three.

359
00:27:19,430 --> 00:27:21,680
Repeat, hill three.

360
00:27:22,140 --> 00:27:24,060
If it's a moving target, 
follow up from there.

361
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:27,600
- All right. Over and out. 
- Number seven, do you hear me?

362
00:27:27,690 --> 00:27:29,940
Anybody get a look at the martians yet?

363
00:27:30,020 --> 00:27:31,860
They've located that second meteor.

364
00:27:35,570 --> 00:27:36,740
Mark it up.

365
00:27:36,860 --> 00:27:40,030
Weapons truck turned over on the road. 
Take action and report.

366
00:27:40,120 --> 00:27:43,450
Company tactical net out of action. 
No radio.

367
00:27:43,540 --> 00:27:46,410
Three, one, eight, zero...

368
00:27:46,500 --> 00:27:50,330
There's one. There's the other. 
And here we are, right between them.

369
00:27:50,500 --> 00:27:52,380
So is the town, I notice.

370
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:54,380
I warned them to be ready 
if they have to evacuate.

371
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:57,470
I just came to tell you 
everyone has been alerted.

372
00:27:57,550 --> 00:27:58,550
As you were.

373
00:27:58,630 --> 00:28:01,300
General mann, I was told 
to expect you, sir. I'm colonel heffner.

374
00:28:01,390 --> 00:28:02,640
I'm here to make up a report,

375
00:28:02,720 --> 00:28:04,310
not to interfere 
with the operations you've setup.

376
00:28:04,350 --> 00:28:05,720
You're still in command.

377
00:28:05,810 --> 00:28:06,930
Clayton forrester.

378
00:28:07,020 --> 00:28:09,730
- I haven't seen you since oak Ridge. 
- Good to see you, general.

379
00:28:09,810 --> 00:28:11,480
- This is pastor Collins. 
- How do you do?

380
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:13,860
- Sheriff bogany, head of local control. 
- General.

381
00:28:13,940 --> 00:28:15,230
- Miss Van buren. 
- Miss Van buren.

382
00:28:15,280 --> 00:28:17,110
How do you do, general? 
Would you care for some coffee?

383
00:28:17,150 --> 00:28:18,360
Thank you.

384
00:28:18,450 --> 00:28:21,450
General mann's in charge 
of intelligence for the pacific area.

385
00:28:23,030 --> 00:28:24,790
That's their position.

386
00:28:24,830 --> 00:28:27,160
Well, you've certainly 
got them surrounded.

387
00:28:27,250 --> 00:28:29,420
I suppose they neutralized 
all communications here.

388
00:28:29,500 --> 00:28:32,380
Not all, sir. Radio's out, 
but our field phones are okay so far.

389
00:28:32,460 --> 00:28:35,250
Well, they'll go the minute 
there's another heat-ray.

390
00:28:35,380 --> 00:28:37,670
"Cylinder reported down 
by huntington beach."

391
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:40,470
- Well, that's a job for the Navy. 
- Any news from abroad?

392
00:28:40,550 --> 00:28:44,100
Washington is in constant touch 
with the military of other nations.

393
00:28:44,220 --> 00:28:46,390
Apparently, they're coming down all over.

394
00:28:46,470 --> 00:28:47,770
South America.

395
00:28:47,850 --> 00:28:49,980
Santiago has two cylinders.

396
00:28:50,020 --> 00:28:52,350
They're outside London.

397
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:54,480
They're in Naples.

398
00:28:54,570 --> 00:28:59,280
We've got them between here and Fresno. 
Outside Sacramento. Two on long island.

399
00:28:59,360 --> 00:29:01,490
They're just coming down at random.

400
00:29:01,570 --> 00:29:03,160
No.

401
00:29:03,240 --> 00:29:05,830
According to information 
from foreign sources,

402
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:08,160
they're working to some kind of a plan.

403
00:29:08,250 --> 00:29:09,870
Now, what it may be isn't clear yet,

404
00:29:10,460 --> 00:29:15,210
simply because, once they begin to move, 
no more news comes out of that area.

405
00:29:19,380 --> 00:29:22,180
We've been getting reports 
of destruction, massacre.

406
00:29:22,260 --> 00:29:23,550
Here's an instance,

407
00:29:23,640 --> 00:29:27,600
"town of St. Julien, south of Bordeaux, 
wiped out by ray of undetermined nature.

408
00:29:27,680 --> 00:29:29,980
"Local reports say nothing remains.”

409
00:29:30,390 --> 00:29:32,140
Nothing remains. What do you make of that?

410
00:29:32,230 --> 00:29:34,150
All we've seen is the heat-ray they use.

411
00:29:34,230 --> 00:29:35,900
Well, some of 
our newest weapons are in here.

412
00:29:35,940 --> 00:29:38,360
- We want to be sure to stop them. 
- We will, sir.

413
00:29:38,650 --> 00:29:39,650
Now, from the data

414
00:29:39,780 --> 00:29:42,860
from that picture 
the air force took earlier tonight,

415
00:29:42,950 --> 00:29:46,490
what we've got out there, 
is the original pilot ship.

416
00:29:46,620 --> 00:29:50,080
On the basis of its observations, 
the others were guided down.

417
00:29:50,160 --> 00:29:53,540
Pattern-wise, one lands, then two,

418
00:29:53,620 --> 00:29:55,880
making groups of threes, 
joined magnetically.

419
00:29:55,960 --> 00:29:57,920
- Is that possible? 
- If they do it, it is.

420
00:29:58,000 --> 00:29:59,380
My orders are not to go into action

421
00:29:59,420 --> 00:30:01,300
unless they move 
out of the shelter of that gully.

422
00:30:01,340 --> 00:30:04,010
Well, meanwhile, 
we'll have a chance to observe them.

423
00:30:04,090 --> 00:30:05,840
This is the only place 
we've had time to surround them

424
00:30:05,890 --> 00:30:07,510
with sufficient force to contain them.

425
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:11,020
What happens here 
will be a guide to all other operations.

426
00:30:11,140 --> 00:30:14,140
Now, the minute action begins 
and a pattern of defense develops,

427
00:30:14,230 --> 00:30:17,520
I'll get my report to Washington.

428
00:30:17,690 --> 00:30:19,980
- You've deployed your forces well. 
- Thank you, sir.

429
00:30:20,070 --> 00:30:23,990
If they start anything, 
we can blast them right off the earth.

430
00:30:32,450 --> 00:30:33,080
They'll probably move at dawn.

431
00:31:15,790 --> 00:31:17,420
There's something moving in the gully.

432
00:31:51,990 --> 00:31:54,370
Look at it, will you?

433
00:31:54,950 --> 00:31:57,960
Beings from another world.

434
00:32:05,590 --> 00:32:08,260
- Is that some kind of a flying machine? 
- No, no.

435
00:32:08,340 --> 00:32:10,050
It's supported from the ground by rays,

436
00:32:10,090 --> 00:32:14,350
probably some form 
of magnetic flux, like invisible legs.

437
00:32:14,430 --> 00:32:17,140
This is amazing.

438
00:32:17,310 --> 00:32:21,360
They must keep the opposing poles 
in balance and lift the machine.

439
00:32:21,900 --> 00:32:24,980
Stand by to fire.

440
00:32:25,110 --> 00:32:27,780
All command posts, stand by to fire.

441
00:32:33,780 --> 00:32:37,450
But, colonel, shooting's no good.

442
00:32:38,080 --> 00:32:41,170
Shouldn't you try to 
communicate with them first,

443
00:32:41,250 --> 00:32:44,000
and shoot later if you have to?

444
00:33:09,860 --> 00:33:13,160
Target as indicated. 
Repeat. Target as indicated.

445
00:33:20,330 --> 00:33:23,040
The 75s report they've got them 
under open sights, sir.

446
00:33:23,130 --> 00:33:25,590
I think we should try 
to make them understand

447
00:33:25,670 --> 00:33:27,460
we mean them no harm.

448
00:33:27,550 --> 00:33:29,510
There's another machine coming out.

449
00:33:37,970 --> 00:33:42,140
- They are living creatures out there. 
- But they're not human.

450
00:33:42,230 --> 00:33:44,690
Dr. forrester says they're some kind 
of advanced civilization.

451
00:33:47,860 --> 00:33:51,570
They should be nearer the creator 
for that reason.

452
00:33:51,740 --> 00:33:55,490
It'll be a moving target. 
Prepare to follow-up.

453
00:33:58,490 --> 00:34:01,080
No real attempt has been made 
to communicate with them, you know?

454
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:04,710
Let's go back inside, uncle Matthew.

455
00:34:06,290 --> 00:34:09,340
I've done all I can in there. You go back.

456
00:34:09,420 --> 00:34:13,300
Sylvia, I like that Dr. forrester.

457
00:34:13,430 --> 00:34:15,840
He's a good man.

458
00:34:15,970 --> 00:34:18,850
Attention, all batteries. 
Prepare for volley fire.

459
00:34:18,930 --> 00:34:22,520
Repeat. Prepare for volley fire.

460
00:34:42,790 --> 00:34:44,120
Who's that?

461
00:34:49,540 --> 00:34:50,840
What's he think he's doing?

462
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:53,220
- Uncle! Uncle Matthew! Stop him! 
- It's too late now. He's too far away.

463
00:34:53,590 --> 00:34:56,510
"Though I walk through the valley 
of the shadow of death,

464
00:34:56,590 --> 00:34:57,680
"t will fear no evil."

465
00:35:02,770 --> 00:35:07,150
"Thou anointest my head with oil. 
My cup runneth over.

466
00:35:12,190 --> 00:35:17,660
"And I will dwell in 
the house of the lord forever."

467
00:35:23,080 --> 00:35:24,660
- Let them have it! 
- Fire!

468
00:35:47,190 --> 00:35:49,190
Those shells can't get through to them.

469
00:35:49,270 --> 00:35:51,820
They've put out some sort 
of electromagnetic covering,

470
00:35:51,900 --> 00:35:53,360
a protective blister.

471
00:37:07,390 --> 00:37:08,430
That skeleton beam must be

472
00:37:08,520 --> 00:37:10,140
what they used 
to wipe out the French city.

473
00:37:10,190 --> 00:37:12,060
It neutralizes mesons somehow.

474
00:37:12,150 --> 00:37:14,520
They're the atomic glue 
holding matter together.

475
00:37:14,610 --> 00:37:18,940
Cut across their lines of magnetic force, 
and any object will simply cease to exist.

476
00:37:19,030 --> 00:37:20,650
Take my word for it, general.

477
00:37:20,860 --> 00:37:24,240
This type of defense is useless 
against that kind of power.

478
00:37:24,370 --> 00:37:26,580
You'd better let Washington know, fast!

479
00:37:28,200 --> 00:37:30,870
- Hold them as long as you can. 
- Right, sir.

480
00:37:54,520 --> 00:37:55,940
Order all command posts.

481
00:37:56,020 --> 00:37:58,650
Everything pull back north 
of highway 60 tunnel bunker.

482
00:37:58,730 --> 00:37:59,820
Right.

483
00:38:29,930 --> 00:38:32,430
Everybody, out of here! Everybody, out!

484
00:38:32,520 --> 00:38:34,730
The air force will take care 
of these babies now.

485
00:38:34,890 --> 00:38:38,150
Dr. forrester, get out of here. 
Everybody out of here!

486
00:38:38,230 --> 00:38:39,730
Everybody...

487
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:11,350
You'll hit something. Can't you go higher?

488
00:39:11,470 --> 00:39:14,600
No. The air will be full 
of jets in a minute. There they are!

489
00:40:04,030 --> 00:40:07,570
All about the martian invasion. 
They're in New York and Miami,

490
00:40:07,650 --> 00:40:09,110
invasion killer rays!

491
00:40:09,200 --> 00:40:12,120
Fighting outside Los Angeles.

492
00:40:12,200 --> 00:40:14,450
All about jt!

493
00:40:33,810 --> 00:40:35,430
Hold it right there, general, please.

494
00:40:35,520 --> 00:40:37,060
You're the gentlemen I asked to come here.

495
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:38,140
- Get Washington. 
- General mann,

496
00:40:38,180 --> 00:40:40,650
what do you think of this situation?

497
00:40:40,730 --> 00:40:42,730
Is it your opinion the army can hold them?

498
00:40:42,810 --> 00:40:43,860
Sorry, I've no time.

499
00:40:43,940 --> 00:40:45,690
You had guns and equipment 
going out there all night.

500
00:40:45,730 --> 00:40:49,820
- Wasn't that enough to... 
- Come on. Break it up.

501
00:40:49,990 --> 00:40:52,320
The way he's hedging, 
maybe the army didn't hold them.

502
00:40:52,410 --> 00:40:56,100
I've seen news off the pacific cables. 
Sydney, Australia. Penang, rangoon, India.

503
00:40:57,080 --> 00:40:59,210
From what's coming through, 
nobody's stopped them yet.

504
00:40:59,250 --> 00:41:01,880
I wanna know 
if the city must be evacuated...

505
00:41:01,960 --> 00:41:04,630
We're ready. Lots of people 
have already moved out.

506
00:41:04,710 --> 00:41:07,170
We've mobilized emergency cars 
and buses in the yards.

507
00:41:07,260 --> 00:41:08,760
The red cross is standing by.

508
00:41:08,840 --> 00:41:12,340
Now, for your information, the enemy 
is 25 or 30 miles outside Los Angeles.

509
00:41:12,430 --> 00:41:14,180
Not down in full force yet, 
but developing.

510
00:41:14,260 --> 00:41:15,680
It can happen any minute.

511
00:41:15,810 --> 00:41:18,890
The crisis will come if they move toward 
the metropolitan area. Now, we've got...

512
00:41:18,930 --> 00:41:21,600
Washington's on the line, sir.

513
00:41:21,770 --> 00:41:22,770
General mann.

514
00:41:25,110 --> 00:41:30,110
I'd say our effective losses 
were nearly 60% men, 90% material.

515
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:33,200
Well, the jets went in, 
but not one of them came out.

516
00:41:33,320 --> 00:41:35,120
I watched high-level bombers 
drop everything they carried.

517
00:41:35,160 --> 00:41:37,290
They were knocked out of the sky, 
and their bombs did nothing.

518
00:41:37,330 --> 00:41:38,580
Nothing was effective against them.

519
00:41:39,580 --> 00:41:41,710
Yes. They have some sort 
of electronic umbrella.

520
00:41:41,790 --> 00:41:43,080
It's quite impenetrable.

521
00:41:43,170 --> 00:41:45,210
Dr. forrester believes 
they generate atomic force

522
00:41:45,340 --> 00:41:46,880
without the heavy screening we use.

523
00:41:46,960 --> 00:41:49,670
That's where they get 
the power for their rays.

524
00:41:51,880 --> 00:41:52,800
Very well, sir.

525
00:41:52,880 --> 00:41:55,260
Call victorville, tell them I want 
the fastest plane they've got.

526
00:41:55,300 --> 00:41:57,600
You'll get all further instructions 
from sixth army command.

527
00:41:57,640 --> 00:41:59,060
Now, I'll make a statement 
to those reporters.

528
00:41:59,100 --> 00:42:00,140
Right.

529
00:42:04,560 --> 00:42:08,400
All right, fellas. 
The general will see you now.

530
00:42:08,480 --> 00:42:09,900
General, we heard

531
00:42:09,980 --> 00:42:12,200
that Dr. Clayton forrester 
was out there with you.

532
00:42:12,280 --> 00:42:13,610
What's he think about this?

533
00:42:13,700 --> 00:42:15,910
Ask him. He's back at pacific tech.

534
00:42:15,990 --> 00:42:18,870
No, he's not. We tried to get him there. 
He hasn't shown up.

535
00:43:19,470 --> 00:43:21,390
Wake up.

536
00:43:26,310 --> 00:43:27,600
Let's get moving, huh?

537
00:43:33,860 --> 00:43:35,110
Are you all right?

538
00:43:36,780 --> 00:43:38,410
- Is that machine... 
- It's gone.

539
00:43:38,490 --> 00:43:41,580
Where are we? 
Southwest of corona somewhere.

540
00:43:41,660 --> 00:43:43,540
There must have been 
another cylinder down here.

541
00:43:43,580 --> 00:43:47,120
They've been through this whole area 
and cleared everybody out.

542
00:43:47,250 --> 00:43:49,210
There's a farmhouse out there.

543
00:43:49,330 --> 00:43:52,300
Let's see if we can 
find something to eat, huh?

544
00:44:00,720 --> 00:44:04,640
We're doing all right. 
You know, I almost forgot when I ate last.

545
00:44:06,890 --> 00:44:10,480
Hey, that looks good.

546
00:44:10,650 --> 00:44:13,610
You know, mostly, I get my meals 
in coffee shops and restaurants.

547
00:44:13,690 --> 00:44:16,030
- Don't you live at home? 
- No, on the campus.

548
00:44:16,110 --> 00:44:20,370
- I haven't any family. 
- I come from a big one.

549
00:44:20,490 --> 00:44:25,330
There's nine of us, 
all in Minnesota, except me.

550
00:44:25,450 --> 00:44:30,000
I have no close folks. 
My parents died when I was a kid.

551
00:44:30,080 --> 00:44:32,710
A big family must be fun. I imagine...

552
00:44:32,840 --> 00:44:34,880
I imagine it makes 
you feel you belong to something.

553
00:44:35,000 --> 00:44:38,380
It does. Maybe that's why I feel 
kind of lost right now.

554
00:44:38,590 --> 00:44:42,350
We'll get out of here safely. 
Don't worry.

555
00:44:43,970 --> 00:44:45,970
But they seem to murder 
everything that moves.

556
00:44:46,640 --> 00:44:50,310
If they're mortal, 
they must have mortal weaknesses.

557
00:44:50,440 --> 00:44:52,770
They'll be stopped. Somehow.

558
00:44:54,400 --> 00:44:55,860
I've been as close to them as anyone,

559
00:44:57,900 --> 00:45:01,410
I feel like I did one time 
when I was small.

560
00:45:01,570 --> 00:45:05,200
Awful scared and lonesome.

561
00:45:05,280 --> 00:45:08,040
I'd wandered off. I've forgotten why.

562
00:45:09,250 --> 00:45:12,750
But the family and whole crowds 
of neighbors were hunting for me.

563
00:45:14,130 --> 00:45:16,250
They found me in a church.

564
00:45:16,380 --> 00:45:20,510
I was afraid to go in anyplace else.

565
00:45:20,680 --> 00:45:22,720
I stayed right by that door,

566
00:45:22,760 --> 00:45:26,850
praying for the one who loved me best 
to come and find me.

567
00:45:31,480 --> 00:45:33,400
It was uncle Matthew who found me.

568
00:45:34,940 --> 00:45:36,270
I liked him.

569
00:45:37,400 --> 00:45:40,400
He liked you.

570
00:45:40,490 --> 00:45:42,110
I could bawl my head off.

571
00:45:42,200 --> 00:45:44,820
But you're not going to. 
You're not the kind.

572
00:45:44,910 --> 00:45:47,580
Look, you're tired. 
You've been up all night.

573
00:45:47,660 --> 00:45:50,870
You cracked up in a plane, 
slept in a ditch.

574
00:45:50,960 --> 00:45:52,330
You want to know something?

575
00:45:54,000 --> 00:45:56,710
It doesn't show on you at all.

576
00:46:08,850 --> 00:46:10,640
Get down!

577
00:46:46,470 --> 00:46:49,680
- How long was I out? 
- Hours. I've been so scared.

578
00:46:49,720 --> 00:46:53,270
They're right outside. 
Several of them came down together.

579
00:47:11,120 --> 00:47:14,040
There's a machine standing 
right alongside of us.

580
00:48:31,780 --> 00:48:35,080
An electronic eye.

581
00:48:35,240 --> 00:48:36,100
Like a television camera.

582
00:48:40,580 --> 00:48:42,040
It's looking for us.

583
00:49:08,650 --> 00:49:12,320
Maybe they're not too sure we're here.

584
00:49:15,870 --> 00:49:19,830
They could be as curious about us 
as we are about them.

585
00:49:19,910 --> 00:49:22,040
Maybe.

586
00:49:23,500 --> 00:49:26,130
Maybe they want to take us alive.

587
00:49:39,730 --> 00:49:40,940
It's pulling out.

588
00:49:59,080 --> 00:50:01,750
Can't we get out of here?

589
00:50:12,220 --> 00:50:14,430
Something moved out there.

590
00:50:16,300 --> 00:50:17,430
There's nothing there now.

591
00:50:17,510 --> 00:50:19,470
It was one of them.

592
00:50:19,680 --> 00:50:20,770
What was it like?

593
00:50:21,020 --> 00:50:23,440
I couldn't see much in the dark, 
but it was one.

594
00:50:23,520 --> 00:50:27,270
We're right in a nest of them. 
I've got to get a good look at them.

595
00:50:37,830 --> 00:50:42,120
- They've blocked it. 
- It's blocked here, too.

596
00:50:42,200 --> 00:50:45,750
They've pushed earth or something, 
all around, outside.

597
00:50:45,880 --> 00:50:49,380
Here. This way. 
The stairway up to the attic.

598
00:52:40,990 --> 00:52:42,530
Your scarf.

599
00:52:50,250 --> 00:52:53,590
We gotta make a run for it. Come on.

600
00:53:00,130 --> 00:53:02,600
The martians had calculated 
their descent upon our earth

601
00:53:02,680 --> 00:53:05,310
with amazing perfection and subtlety.

602
00:53:05,640 --> 00:53:09,350
As more of their cylinders came 
from the mysterious depths of space,

603
00:53:09,440 --> 00:53:13,650
their war machines, 
awesome in their power and complexity,

604
00:53:13,730 --> 00:53:17,690
created a wave of fear, which swept 
into all corners of the world.

605
00:53:17,780 --> 00:53:21,070
In every country, government officials 
met in desperate conclave,

606
00:53:21,160 --> 00:53:24,830
seeking ways to coordinate their defenses 
with those of other nations.

607
00:53:27,660 --> 00:53:29,660
The government of India, 
driven from New Delhi,

608
00:53:29,750 --> 00:53:31,460
met in a railroad coach,

609
00:53:31,620 --> 00:53:34,080
while massive hindu populations streamed

610
00:53:34,170 --> 00:53:37,550
for the imagined safety 
of the faraway himalayas.

611
00:53:38,130 --> 00:53:40,420
The redoubtable Finnish 
and turkish armies,

612
00:53:40,510 --> 00:53:44,970
Chinese battalions and Bolivians 
worked and fought furiously.

613
00:53:45,470 --> 00:53:47,770
Every effort against the tremendous power

614
00:53:47,850 --> 00:53:51,560
of their other-world antagonists 
ended in the same frantic rout.

615
00:53:55,520 --> 00:53:59,820
As the martians burned fields and forests, 
and great cities fell before them,

616
00:53:59,900 --> 00:54:02,820
huge populations were 
driven from their homes.

617
00:54:02,990 --> 00:54:06,080
The stream of flight 
Rose swiftly to a torrent.

618
00:54:06,160 --> 00:54:10,370
It became a giant stampede 
without order and without goal.

619
00:54:10,660 --> 00:54:13,620
It was the beginning 
of the rout of civilization,

620
00:54:13,710 --> 00:54:15,630
of the massacre of humanity.

621
00:54:21,760 --> 00:54:26,640
A great silence fell over half of Europe 
as all communication was disrupted.

622
00:54:28,760 --> 00:54:30,520
When the last wire photo out of Paris

623
00:54:30,600 --> 00:54:33,350
reached the French cabinet 
exiled in strasbourg,

624
00:54:33,440 --> 00:54:37,230
they hit upon the idea 
of using super-speed jets as couriers.

625
00:54:37,310 --> 00:54:40,230
Stripped of armament 
and loaded with extra fuel,

626
00:54:40,320 --> 00:54:43,400
these planes maintained connection 
with the Scandinavian countries,

627
00:54:43,490 --> 00:54:47,780
north Africa, the United States, 
and especially with england.

628
00:54:47,910 --> 00:54:49,990
It was plain the martians appreciated

629
00:54:50,080 --> 00:54:53,620
the strategic significance 
of the British isles.

630
00:54:54,250 --> 00:54:57,460
The people of britain met 
the invaders magnificently.

631
00:54:57,580 --> 00:54:59,210
But it was unavailing.

632
00:55:00,710 --> 00:55:02,670
As the martians swept 
northward toward London,

633
00:55:02,760 --> 00:55:04,840
the British cabinet stayed in session,

634
00:55:04,930 --> 00:55:08,930
coordinating every item of information 
that could be gathered,

635
00:55:09,010 --> 00:55:12,390
passing it on 
to the united nations in New York.

636
00:55:12,520 --> 00:55:15,140
From there, the news was 
forwarded to Washington

637
00:55:15,230 --> 00:55:19,610
because here was the only remaining 
unassailed strategic point.

638
00:55:19,690 --> 00:55:21,730
Forty-eight north. Two west.

639
00:55:21,820 --> 00:55:22,940
Thirty-one east.

640
00:55:23,030 --> 00:55:24,740
- Anchored on Cairo. 
- Checking.

641
00:55:24,820 --> 00:55:26,950
Dover blanked out.

642
00:55:27,030 --> 00:55:28,240
Brazil checking.

643
00:55:28,320 --> 00:55:32,160
Urgent. Major Bentley Dawson, 
report to general mann.

644
00:55:36,870 --> 00:55:39,210
Eighty west by 38 north.

645
00:55:40,090 --> 00:55:42,670
Make every effort to contact 
the civil authorities,

646
00:55:42,760 --> 00:55:45,090
evacuating declared emergency area.

647
00:55:48,970 --> 00:55:50,220
And this much is certain,

648
00:55:52,220 --> 00:55:55,270
it's vital to prevent 
the martian machines from linking up.

649
00:55:55,350 --> 00:55:59,400
Once they do, they adopt 
an extraordinary military tactic.

650
00:55:59,520 --> 00:56:01,520
They form a Crescent.

651
00:56:01,690 --> 00:56:06,110
They anchor it at one end and sweep on,

652
00:56:06,200 --> 00:56:07,570
until they've cleared a quadrant.

653
00:56:08,780 --> 00:56:13,870
Then they anchor the opposite end 
and reverse direction.

654
00:56:13,990 --> 00:56:16,620
They slash across country like scythes,

655
00:56:16,710 --> 00:56:19,040
wiping out everything that's trying 
to get away from them.

656
00:56:19,120 --> 00:56:22,630
That explains why communication is cut 
the moment their machines begin moving.

657
00:56:22,750 --> 00:56:25,710
Montreal's blacked out. 
Nothing more has come through.

658
00:56:25,800 --> 00:56:27,590
Same thing that happened 
on the pacific coast.

659
00:56:27,670 --> 00:56:29,430
- Anything from them yet? 
- No, Mr. secretary.

660
00:56:29,470 --> 00:56:32,100
We've had nothing 
from San Francisco for over five hours.

661
00:56:32,180 --> 00:56:35,560
Excuse me, gentlemen. 
Here's that Los Angeles picture, sir.

662
00:56:35,640 --> 00:56:38,850
This is a Sonic radar picture 
taken from extreme altitude.

663
00:56:38,940 --> 00:56:42,730
It shows full details of the martian nest 
outside Los Angeles.

664
00:56:42,900 --> 00:56:48,490
Their machines appear as round blobs. 
Newly-fallen cylinders are elongated.

665
00:56:49,660 --> 00:56:52,240
We know there are three cylinders 
to each group

666
00:56:52,320 --> 00:56:54,370
and three machines to each cylinder.

667
00:57:00,290 --> 00:57:02,750
All right. I've seen enough.

668
00:57:04,840 --> 00:57:07,170
There's only one thing 
that'll stop the martians.

669
00:57:07,260 --> 00:57:10,630
We've held back because 
of the danger of radiation to civilians.

670
00:57:10,720 --> 00:57:12,970
Now, there's no choice.

671
00:57:13,140 --> 00:57:16,100
The white house will confirm an order 
to use the atom bomb.

672
00:57:16,180 --> 00:57:20,270
Then our first target will be the initial 
landing place outside Los Angeles.

673
00:57:20,390 --> 00:57:21,650
First thing you get there,

674
00:57:21,730 --> 00:57:24,060
arrange for the pacific tech scientists 
to monitor the drop.

675
00:57:24,110 --> 00:57:28,320
I'll send wire orders ahead of you, 
and have the area cleared all around.

676
00:57:30,650 --> 00:57:34,070
We've still time to hit them before dark.

677
00:57:34,200 --> 00:57:37,040
Then we'll blast them all over the world.

678
00:57:37,160 --> 00:57:41,500
Pacific institute 
of science and technology

679
00:57:43,920 --> 00:57:45,540
forrester, everybody's been 
looking for you.

680
00:57:45,590 --> 00:57:49,670
I know. We've walked halfway from corona. 
Finally found an abandoned truck.

681
00:57:49,760 --> 00:57:51,590
This is miss Van buren.

682
00:57:51,680 --> 00:57:54,850
Dr. gratzman, Dr. pryor, 
James, bilderbeck.

683
00:57:54,930 --> 00:57:56,220
What's this I hear about the a-bomb?

684
00:57:56,260 --> 00:57:57,680
We've been assigned as special detail.

685
00:57:57,720 --> 00:57:59,470
We're going in right afterwards, 
study its effect.

686
00:57:59,520 --> 00:58:01,730
We're leaving in half an hour. 
What's that?

687
00:58:01,810 --> 00:58:05,020
- King-sized fisheye? 
- This is an electronic eye.

688
00:58:05,190 --> 00:58:07,020
The martians modeled it 
after their own eye.

689
00:58:07,110 --> 00:58:09,780
They use it the way we use a periscope.

690
00:58:09,860 --> 00:58:11,820
It'll tell us a lot about 
their metals and alloys.

691
00:58:11,900 --> 00:58:15,320
If this is actually a lens, 
we can find out about their optics.

692
00:58:15,410 --> 00:58:19,580
- Interesting. Very interesting. 
- And this...

693
00:58:19,750 --> 00:58:21,370
Blood of a martian.

694
00:58:23,670 --> 00:58:28,380
I don't remember ever seeing 
blood crystals as anemic as these.

695
00:58:29,250 --> 00:58:32,220
They may be mental giants,

696
00:58:32,260 --> 00:58:34,470
but by our standards,

697
00:58:34,510 --> 00:58:38,220
physically, they must be very primitive.

698
00:58:38,600 --> 00:58:42,730
Isn't it curious how everything 
about them seems to be in threes?

699
00:58:42,810 --> 00:58:45,440
Their eyes have three lenses, 
three distinct pupils,

700
00:58:45,520 --> 00:58:48,440
- strong light shocks them. 
- They're not accustomed to it.

701
00:58:48,520 --> 00:58:52,650
Sunlight on Mars is approximately 
half as strong as we get it.

702
00:58:52,740 --> 00:58:57,370
Add their clouds and dust, 
it amounts to no more than our twilight.

703
00:58:59,200 --> 00:59:02,620
Now, if you'll step over here, please.

704
00:59:02,700 --> 00:59:04,330
We've rigged the epidiascope

705
00:59:04,410 --> 00:59:07,380
to reflect whatever 
the martian lens picks up.

706
00:59:11,380 --> 00:59:14,300
Move in a little. Thank you.

707
00:59:46,830 --> 00:59:48,540
There's how the martians see us.

708
01:00:01,970 --> 01:00:06,890
Evidently, there's a shift 
in their spectrum.

709
01:00:06,980 --> 01:00:09,690
Their color absorption 
must be different from ours.

710
01:00:14,610 --> 01:00:21,370
Let's see why they were 
so curious about you, miss Van buren.

711
01:00:28,500 --> 01:00:31,540
Time to get started, gentlemen.

712
01:00:31,670 --> 01:00:33,170
Let's go.

713
01:00:35,260 --> 01:00:36,380
That martian blood,

714
01:00:36,510 --> 01:00:39,300
let them make a quick analysis of it 
to see what we've got.

715
01:00:39,380 --> 01:00:40,760
It might give us something.

716
01:00:40,840 --> 01:00:42,350
- Something we could use. 
- Let it go.

717
01:00:42,470 --> 01:00:43,850
If you're interested in martian blood,

718
01:00:43,890 --> 01:00:48,140
you'll get all you want 
right after the plane drops the bomb.

719
01:00:48,310 --> 01:00:50,980
The flying wing is going to carry it.

720
01:01:00,410 --> 01:01:03,240
Hello, tower, this is 
air force flying wing ready for takeoff.

721
01:01:03,330 --> 01:01:04,450
Over.

722
01:01:04,530 --> 01:01:08,120
Flying wing, this is the tower. 
Clear for takeoff. Over.

723
01:01:08,250 --> 01:01:11,000
Wish us luck. Roger.

724
01:01:37,480 --> 01:01:41,860
The target for the a-bomb is this nest 
of martian machines in the puente hills,

725
01:01:41,990 --> 01:01:45,870
where more of these meteor-cylinders 
came down early last evening.

726
01:01:45,950 --> 01:01:49,580
A plane will pinpoint the target 
for the drop from six miles up.

727
01:01:49,830 --> 01:01:53,170
We've been warned that this bomb 
is 10 times more powerful

728
01:01:53,250 --> 01:01:55,290
than anything previously used.

729
01:01:55,380 --> 01:01:57,750
It's the latest thing in nuclear fission.

730
01:01:57,840 --> 01:02:00,760
Nothing like this has ever 
been exploded before,

731
01:02:00,800 --> 01:02:02,840
and we're going to be pretty darn close.

732
01:02:02,930 --> 01:02:06,300
But there are observers 
down in the valley in a forward bunker,

733
01:02:06,390 --> 01:02:08,520
they'll be a lot nearer than us.

734
01:02:08,680 --> 01:02:10,730
The whole world is waiting,

735
01:02:10,810 --> 01:02:15,310
for this will decide 
the fate of civilization and all humanity.

736
01:02:16,060 --> 01:02:19,900
Whether we live or die, 
may depend on what happens here.

737
01:02:19,990 --> 01:02:24,490
Attention, please. 
Four minutes to bomb time.

738
01:02:25,870 --> 01:02:27,450
There must be a couple of million people

739
01:02:27,490 --> 01:02:30,660
back of us in the shelter 
of the San Gabriel hills.

740
01:02:30,750 --> 01:02:34,920
Waiting. Waiting to find out 
whether they can go home again.

741
01:02:35,290 --> 01:02:39,000
Everywhere, all around the world, 
people have been driven from their homes.

742
01:02:39,090 --> 01:02:42,590
Direct cable communication 
is being maintained with Washington,

743
01:02:42,670 --> 01:02:44,720
but there's no radio at all,

744
01:02:44,800 --> 01:02:48,510
not even with the 
bombing plane that's coming over.

745
01:02:48,600 --> 01:02:51,890
All radio is dead.

746
01:02:51,930 --> 01:02:54,480
Which means that these 
tape recordings I'm making

747
01:02:54,560 --> 01:02:57,610
are for the sake of future history.

748
01:02:57,690 --> 01:02:58,820
If any.

749
01:03:00,150 --> 01:03:04,360
Future history, he said.

750
01:03:03,610 --> 01:03:06,070
Yes. If any.

751
01:03:16,210 --> 01:03:19,130
Bilderbeck has calculated 
how long we have got

752
01:03:19,210 --> 01:03:21,510
until martians take over the entire world.

753
01:03:21,590 --> 01:03:23,970
If the a-bomb fails, that is,

754
01:03:24,260 --> 01:03:28,010
the martians can conquer the earth 
in six days.

755
01:03:28,930 --> 01:03:31,180
The same number of days 
it took to create it.

756
01:03:38,100 --> 01:03:41,980
Attention, please. 
Two minutes to bomb time.

757
01:03:42,070 --> 01:03:43,860
Prepare to take shelter.

758
01:03:45,860 --> 01:03:48,530
If you have no goggles, turn away.

759
01:03:48,620 --> 01:03:52,870
Remember, the heat flash 
and concussion that follow are dangerous.

760
01:04:08,300 --> 01:04:09,640
Fifty seconds.

761
01:04:12,510 --> 01:04:14,390
- There's the plane. 
- Right.

762
01:04:16,060 --> 01:04:17,980
Forty seconds.

763
01:04:20,860 --> 01:04:23,780
- We've spotted the plane, sir. 
- Right.

764
01:04:25,990 --> 01:04:27,400
Thirty seconds.

765
01:04:27,490 --> 01:04:30,570
Look. Look!

766
01:04:31,830 --> 01:04:33,950
They're using 
those protective blisters again.

767
01:04:34,040 --> 01:04:35,290
Twenty seconds.

768
01:04:35,540 --> 01:04:37,960
Attention. Stand by.

769
01:04:38,040 --> 01:04:40,500
Fifteen seconds.

770
01:04:43,040 --> 01:04:44,590
Ten seconds.

771
01:04:44,670 --> 01:04:48,680
Nine, eight, seven,

772
01:04:48,760 --> 01:04:52,640
six, five, four,

773
01:04:52,720 --> 01:04:56,390
three, two, one.

774
01:05:29,300 --> 01:05:32,550
Hello! Hello, hello, there. 
What can you see?

775
01:05:42,600 --> 01:05:43,940
There's something moving.

776
01:05:55,490 --> 01:05:57,410
They haven't even been touched!

777
01:06:07,960 --> 01:06:10,050
It didn't stop them.

778
01:06:10,130 --> 01:06:13,220
Guns, tanks, bombs. 
They're like toys against them.

779
01:06:13,930 --> 01:06:17,010
It'll end only one way. We're beaten.

780
01:06:18,720 --> 01:06:21,770
No. Not yet.

781
01:06:24,190 --> 01:06:25,770
Washington issued orders.

782
01:06:25,860 --> 01:06:28,690
In the event the a-bomb failed, 
evacuate all cities in danger of attack.

783
01:06:29,070 --> 01:06:29,610
They'll be moving on Los Angeles now.

784
01:06:30,610 --> 01:06:36,120
We'll establish a line and fight them 
all the way back to the mountains.

785
01:06:36,240 --> 01:06:38,660
Our best hope lies in what you people 
can develop to help us.

786
01:06:40,250 --> 01:06:43,290
All right, on the double. Get me back 
to sixth army hq. Hurry up!

787
01:06:43,370 --> 01:06:47,790
Six days, you said. Six days.

788
01:06:47,920 --> 01:06:49,800
They'll stamp the city flat.

789
01:06:53,800 --> 01:06:55,050
We'll take all our instruments

790
01:06:55,140 --> 01:06:57,800
and establish a base laboratory 
in the rocky mountains.

791
01:06:57,890 --> 01:07:00,430
It'll give us time to search out 
some weakness in the martians.

792
01:07:00,520 --> 01:07:02,930
A forlorn hope, but there is a chance.

793
01:07:03,020 --> 01:07:05,520
We may get a lead from that anemic blood.

794
01:07:05,730 --> 01:07:07,980
You mean by some biological approach?

795
01:07:08,060 --> 01:07:12,150
We know now, 
that we can't beat their machines.

796
01:07:12,280 --> 01:07:14,240
We've got to beat them.

797
01:07:29,380 --> 01:07:34,470
Everybody, listen carefully. 
The martians are coming this way.

798
01:07:34,510 --> 01:07:36,430
We must evacuate the city.

799
01:07:36,970 --> 01:07:39,850
Take food and water 
and extra clothing with you.

800
01:07:40,350 --> 01:07:42,850
All major highways have been marked

801
01:07:42,930 --> 01:07:46,060
to lead you to shelter 
and welfare centers in the hills.

802
01:07:55,110 --> 01:07:58,780
Come on, come on. Keep going.

803
01:07:59,200 --> 01:08:01,450
Keep going.

804
01:08:07,500 --> 01:08:10,090
Keep them rolling, there. Keep going.

805
01:08:10,710 --> 01:08:11,880
Come on.

806
01:08:11,920 --> 01:08:12,920
Come on!

807
01:09:20,240 --> 01:09:22,410
Gratzman, did you get those biotics?

808
01:09:22,490 --> 01:09:24,200
No, I thought you had them!

809
01:09:24,280 --> 01:09:26,910
Never mind. I'll get them! 
You get in the school bus!

810
01:09:26,100 --> 01:09:28,870
Sylvia will drive you!

811
01:10:48,410 --> 01:10:50,410
Let me up.

812
01:10:50,500 --> 01:10:53,830
- Get off, buster! 
- I'll give you $500 for your place.

813
01:10:53,920 --> 01:10:57,340
- I'll make it $1,000! 
- Money's no good anymore, Mac!

814
01:11:04,470 --> 01:11:06,890
Here comes another truck!

815
01:11:07,300 --> 01:11:10,600
- Throw that guy off there! 
- Get him out of therel

816
01:11:11,350 --> 01:11:12,560
stop, you fools!

817
01:11:12,640 --> 01:11:13,730
Get over!

818
01:11:14,520 --> 01:11:16,440
You fools!

819
01:11:40,920 --> 01:11:42,550
Quick! Help me! We've got to stop them!

820
01:11:42,630 --> 01:11:44,420
We can't stop them. 
The law's no good here now.

821
01:11:44,470 --> 01:11:46,720
They're thieves, robbers, worse. 
They wouldn't leave with the rest.

822
01:11:46,760 --> 01:11:49,050
- It's like this all over. 
- They've got to be stopped!

823
01:11:49,140 --> 01:11:50,470
Those instruments!

824
01:11:57,350 --> 01:11:59,810
Hold it! Hold it a minute!

825
01:12:01,860 --> 01:12:05,240
We've got to have those instruments. 
They're our only chance!

826
01:12:30,390 --> 01:12:33,180
Did that mob grab the trucks ahead of me?

827
01:12:33,260 --> 01:12:35,980
There were a lot of pacific tech people 
with those trucks. Did you see them?

828
01:12:36,020 --> 01:12:37,640
I don't know anything about other trucks.

829
01:12:37,730 --> 01:12:39,270
There's been fighting on all the streets.

830
01:12:39,310 --> 01:12:41,480
A school bus. 
A girl was driving. Did they get that?

831
01:12:41,560 --> 01:12:44,030
If they saw it, they took it. 
They'll grab anything on wheels.

832
01:12:44,400 --> 01:12:47,610
You can't buy a ride for love or money!

833
01:13:43,080 --> 01:13:45,630
School bus

834
01:13:54,010 --> 01:13:57,600
Gratzman! Bilderbeck!

835
01:13:58,220 --> 01:14:01,190
Sylvia!

836
01:14:31,670 --> 01:14:33,680
Hey, you, better get out of here.

837
01:14:33,760 --> 01:14:36,260
I'm looking for some 
pacific tech professors!

838
01:14:36,350 --> 01:14:39,140
- There's nobody left around here now! 
- We had a chance.

839
01:14:39,220 --> 01:14:40,980
We could've stopped them.

840
01:14:41,060 --> 01:14:44,480
The mob stole all the trucks, 
smashed everything up.

841
01:14:44,560 --> 01:14:46,560
Fools! They cut their own throats!

842
01:14:47,110 --> 01:14:50,030
He's nuts. Come on. Jump in.

843
01:15:01,870 --> 01:15:04,500
- Hurry up! Jump in! 
- There was a girl with them.

844
01:15:04,580 --> 01:15:07,340
- If I can find them... 
- Come on. Jump in, will you?

845
01:15:07,420 --> 01:15:12,340
- She's kind of lost. 
- You look kind of lost yourself.

846
01:15:12,380 --> 01:15:14,680
But I think it know where she'll be.

847
01:15:15,010 --> 01:15:18,260
Come on, come on. 
It's your last chance to get out of here.

848
01:16:12,610 --> 01:16:17,490
We humbly beseech 
thy divine guidance, o lord.

849
01:16:17,570 --> 01:16:21,870
Deliver us from the fear 
which has come upon us.

850
01:16:21,990 --> 01:16:27,080
From the evil that grows ever nearer.

851
01:16:27,210 --> 01:16:33,750
From the terror that soon will knock 
upon the very door of this, thy house.

852
01:16:37,220 --> 01:16:41,050
O lord, we pray thee,

853
01:16:41,140 --> 01:16:45,430
Grant us the miracle 
of thy divine intervention.

854
01:16:51,150 --> 01:16:53,770
Don't go, son. Stay with us.

855
01:16:53,860 --> 01:16:59,110
No. I'm looking for someone. 
She'll be in a church, near the door.

856
01:18:05,350 --> 01:18:06,680
Forrester!

857
01:18:06,850 --> 01:18:08,600
Duprey, bilderbeck!

858
01:18:08,680 --> 01:18:10,060
Are you...

859
01:18:11,390 --> 01:18:12,400
Where are the others?

860
01:18:13,230 --> 01:18:16,690
A mob attacked us. 
We don't know what happened to the rest.

861
01:18:17,730 --> 01:18:19,490
I... I got...

862
01:18:19,570 --> 01:18:22,150
I got knocked under our truck.

863
01:18:25,450 --> 01:18:27,080
Duprey pulled me out.

864
01:18:28,240 --> 01:18:29,450
What happened to Sylvia?

865
01:18:30,290 --> 01:18:32,290
We never did see her again.

866
01:19:09,660 --> 01:19:14,370
In our peril, we plead, 
succor and comfort us in this hour.

867
01:19:16,460 --> 01:19:17,750
Please, god.

868
01:19:33,230 --> 01:19:34,890
Sylvia!

869
01:19:45,950 --> 01:19:47,620
Sylvia!

870
01:19:54,870 --> 01:19:55,870
Sylvia!

871
01:19:59,590 --> 01:20:02,800
- Here! Here! 
- Sylvia!

872
01:20:12,970 --> 01:20:16,310
Let me through, please!

873
01:21:14,450 --> 01:21:16,080
Maybe they've gone another way.

874
01:21:26,630 --> 01:21:28,090
Let's go see.

875
01:21:31,180 --> 01:21:32,550
It must be all right.

876
01:22:41,370 --> 01:22:42,710
Something's happening to them.

877
01:23:03,060 --> 01:23:04,520
It's dead.

878
01:23:21,450 --> 01:23:24,500
We were all praying for a miracle.

879
01:23:37,050 --> 01:23:40,390
The martians had no resistance 
to the bacteria in our atmosphere

880
01:23:40,470 --> 01:23:43,180
to which we have long since become immune.

881
01:23:43,310 --> 01:23:47,190
Once they had breathed our air, 
germs which no longer affect us

882
01:23:47,270 --> 01:23:48,940
began to kill them.

883
01:23:49,020 --> 01:23:51,070
The end came swiftly.

884
01:23:51,190 --> 01:23:55,030
All over the world, their machines 
began to stop and fall.

885
01:23:56,030 --> 01:23:58,780
After all that men could do had failed,

886
01:23:58,870 --> 01:24:02,080
the martians were destroyed 
and humanity was saved

887
01:24:02,450 --> 01:24:04,290
by the littlest things

888
01:24:04,460 --> 01:24:07,580
which god, in his wisdom, 
had put upon this earth.
